# (thresh)Hold The Line — advances in VTSearch calibration.
# Mildly technical audience with no prior exposure to VTSearch: the opening
# third builds the tool, and lands on the one object the whole talk is about —
# a line through a ranking that decides twice, what comes back and what you are
# asked next. That first third is deliberately click-by-click — make a
# detector, answer questions, run it over media nobody voted on — because a
# room that has not seen the tool cannot be told about a threshold inside it.
#
# Then the ideas, in order: the three the line is built from — held-out
# scores, the unlabeled corpus, and the mixture fit — and the fit that joins
# them, then the axis the *user* sets — F-beta, the three-radio balance that asks which mistake they mind
# more (#4413, #4448), and the line the labels draw at its peak (#4452). Then
# the places the loop never asks about, the steps Autopilot takes and when it
# says stop (#4610), the data the experiments run on and how
# the app does on it, how a user tests a detector on
# their own pile and how often that test is right, the region-voting idea (whose
# measured verdict waits for the runs still reporting), documents — logos on
# scanned pages — ending on how the app does on them, and faces: the consented
# benchmark the app's face path is measured on (#4699).
#
# The deck is ideas, not experiments: it presents findings, and the studies
# behind them live in their reports, not on the slides or in the notes (see
# #3246, #3265, #4827).
#
# The outline fragment appears thirteen times: once bare, as the opening list,
# and then once before each section marked with `+at1` … `+at11`. It is one
# slide shown thirteen ways rather than thirteen slides — `build.py` numbers it
# 1a…1m — and the last showing, `+end`, is the deck's closing slide: it is the
# only one that shows "Conclusion", as its marked line. Sections are numbered in
# Roman numerals (I … XI), slides in Arabic, so the two never read alike.
#
# This is the master deck: every fragment a talk might want is named here, and
# a talk cuts its own manifest from it. Section XI, Instruction, is the one most
# talks cut — it is a how-to for users, not an argument for data scientists — and
# a talk *for* users moves it up to follow section I instead.

theme: vtsearch
# PDF metadata. The title is set here rather than left to Marp: it takes one
# from the first heading, and the speaker cut has none (its first page is a
# picture of the title slide), so that PDF was titled after Marp's temp file
# (#4835); `build.py` adds "(presenter notes)" to the speaker cut's. The
# language Marp guesses from the locale of whatever machine renders it
# (`POSIX` on a CI runner), and that is what a screen reader reads the deck in.
title: (thresh)Hold The Line
author: Sam Greenberg
lang: en

slides:
  title-hold-the-line
  outline-hold-the-line

  # I — Mission: the tool, from nothing, and what it is for.
  outline-hold-the-line +at1
  problem
  make-detector
  train-loop
  find-results
  # Drawing the Line, the Find screen scrolled to the line, was cut here
  # (owner, 2026-10-13: too technical this early, and the Test flow has moved
  # on since it was shot); the line is first met on Pics on a Plane.
  embed-flow
  vote-boundary

  # II — Calibration: how to threshold a model without reusing the data that
  # trained it. The three ideas the labels line (section III) is built from,
  # and only those (#4670): the folds' held-out scores are its class model; the
  # unlabeled corpus is where it counts the matches; EM is how it fits that
  # corpus. Then three ways of putting them together, naive to nuanced (owner,
  # 2026-10-13, #4833): Cross Examination averages the folds' cut with the
  # corpus's; Above Average reads the votes and the corpus's histograms
  # together, the votes only naming the bumps; Curvey and InDiscreet (once
  # titled for a custody split; owner, #4835) lets the votes, bunched near the line where
  # Autopilot asks, shape the Good bump and the corpus say how big it is,
  # ending on the two numbers the line is drawn from. It uses no beta, so it is here
  # rather than beside the line it feeds: turning it into a line takes the
  # user's preference, which is section III. The other rules that once
  # stacked on these — the anchored EM, rank transfer, the 70/30 split — are in
  # the appendix: measured in F-beta, none of them beat cross-calibration
  # (#4582, #4583), and none draws today's line.
  outline-hold-the-line +at2
  xcal
  gmm-idea
  gmm-em
  blend
  anchored-gmm
  labels-fit

  # III — Inclination: the user's preference, worked into the line (owner,
  # #4833: first define the preference, then use it to calibrate). Why a line
  # needs one, what F1 and F-beta are (#4517), F-beta's peak and the
  # three-radio balance that sets its beta (#4413, #4448), then the line the
  # labels draw at its expected peak (#4452), from section II's Curvey and
  # InDiscreet fit. Then why the labels are what the line is drawn from: they
  # are all Find has (#4517). Then
  # the line before the labels can draw one, the outlier cut, and
  # the map of which rule draws it for every labelset (#4603, #4759, #4786,
  # #4798). Changing Hands, the same hand-over as a timeline, was cut: Lay of
  # the Land covers it (owner, #4835). The Spot Check slides that followed
  # moved to Examination: they measure the line, they do not draw it (owner,
  # #4835). Present
  # tense throughout: the deck says what the app does, and the slider that
  # came before the balance is in the appendix after Conclusion.
  # Second Cut stays last: Autopilot asks where What to Expect's fit puts
  # even odds (#3546, #4632), so it has to follow the slides that draw that
  # fit and say where the line lands beside it.
  outline-hold-the-line +at3
  inclusion-rank
  fbeta-metrics
  fbeta-maps
  balance-fbeta
  labels-line
  transfer-student
  # The line before the labels line can stand: the outlier cut,
  # drawn by one rule for the text query, the Goods' average and a head with
  # too few Goods (#4603, #4759, #4786): needles are rare and a first sort is
  # weak, so the matches are a few outliers above the bulk, not a hump (owner,
  # #4835). After Transfer Student, because both slides lean on the radios
  # and on What to Expect's fit to say what the count stands in for.
  out-standing
  # Lay of the Land: every labelset in hand, Goods up and Bads across: the quota, the text query's
  # hold, and the band where the folds' split decides, from the shipped quota, fold split and hold
  # (#4643, #4731, #4736). Inside Job, the same rule as a decision tree on what each fold holds out,
  # was cut: the room can read the cut rules off this map (owner, #4833).
  lay-of-the-land
  inclusion-acq

  # IV — Exploration: the Coverage Atlas, how autopilot decides what to ask
  # when the answer is not "whatever is nearest the line". It follows Inclination
  # because Second Cut has just said the threshold decides what gets asked, and
  # the honest reading of these four is "and here is the half of that the
  # threshold does not do".
  #
  # The order is the argument, not the code: the blind spot the atlas exists
  # for, the structure, the second job that structure also does, the atlas
  # doing it on that very room (#4517), and how badly it does it. That last one stays last whatever happens to the rest — it is
  # the only slide here that reports a loss, and a loss read before the
  # mechanism is just a reason not to listen.
  outline-hold-the-line +at4
  atlas-blindspot
  atlas-cells
  atlas-depth
  atlas-far
  atlas-pvalues

  # V — Navigation: Autopilot from end to end (#4610). Sections III and IV are
  # the two ways it asks; this is the order it asks them in, and when it tells
  # the user they can stop. The steps first, then one slide per light — Smart,
  # Stable, Span, each drawn by the shipped rule on a planted session
  # (`make-autopilot-figs.py`) — then the ways a run ends and what the app says.
  # Last, where the stop fired when it was measured: #3945's sessions, the only
  # stopping numbers committed so far, until a State of the App review's
  # stopping block replaces them (#4611).
  outline-hold-the-line +at5
  autopilot-steps
  autopilot-smart
  autopilot-stable
  autopilot-span
  autopilot-done
  autopilot-stop

  # VI — Experimentation: the photographs the experiments run on. Every dataset
  # arrives on one card, "Data, Set" (`make-data-cards.py`): the sets somebody
  # else made, a slide of what they do not give you, then what we built from
  # them, in the same card — COCO into COCO Better (`coco_better` in code;
  # the slides use the name a person would say).
  #
  # The figures are generated from the config the pile builds against, so a
  # rebuilt dataset is a re-run of the figure scripts rather than an edit here —
  # but the presenter notes carry numbers too, and those are hand-written.
  outline-hold-the-line +at6
  data-set-coco
  data-coco-better-complement
  data-coco-problems
  data-set-coco-better
  # Up and Up: what the F-beta era bought at beta 1/4, in two lifts (#4668).
  up-and-up
  # Follow Suit, what the work since the first rule bought (#4548), is in the
  # appendix after Conclusion: only β ¼ pulls away from cross-calibration, so
  # in the run of the talk it read as "the work changed nothing" (#4563).
  # How the app does on that bench today: the State of the App review, each
  # radio on its own sessions (`make-sota-figs.py`, from the report's tables).
  sota-photos

  # VII — Examination: the Test button (#4594, #4598), how a user finds out
  # what section VI just measured, on their own pile. It follows Experimentation
  # because its last slide grades the Test on the COCO Better sessions the room
  # has just met. The question and its two rules; the background, one idea a
  # slide — stratified bands, one band's Beta posterior, the joint Monte Carlo
  # draws, the prior borrowed from neighbouring bands above the line (#4539,
  # #4560) and the model's count corrected below it (#4523); then the app — the
  # autopilot's rounds and stop, the result pane, and how often its ranges hold.
  # The bands and Clopper–Pearson it builds on are Spot Check's and Home on the
  # Range's, which open the section. Every figure but the last runs the shipped
  # `LineTest` on a planted pile (`make-test-figs.py`); the last is #4560's
  # shipped summary.
  outline-hold-the-line +at7
  # First the check Autopilot runs on the pile you trained on (owner, #4835:
  # moved here from Inclination, since it measures the line rather than
  # drawing it): the question the line cannot answer before anyone checks
  # (how many of what it keeps are right), Clopper–Pearson on its own, what
  # a check says about the line without moving it (#4427, #4452, #4517).
  # Flight Plan in section V names it as the step before Done (#4836).
  floor-ask
  clopper-pearson
  floor-check
  # Weak Spot: when the labels separate weakly, Autopilot runs Spot Check
  # early (#4496), past the opening and never on faces (#4799). After Spot
  # Check, which it calls. Then Test, the same question on a pile the
  # detector never saw.
  weak-spot
  test-question
  test-bands
  test-posterior
  test-draws
  test-pool
  test-model
  test-rounds
  test-result
  test-coverage

  # VIII — Region: the region vote in the app, what a patch is and the model
  # that makes them (DINOv3, #4517), and the maximum a photo is then scored by.
  outline-hold-the-line +at8
  voting-modes
  patches
  evt-gumbel
  # How the app does on the region path today: the State of the App review
  # of 2026-10-06 (#4534), drawn with Photo Finish's two panels.
  sota-regions

  # IX — Document(ation): logos on scanned pages. The logo slide opens it
  # because it introduces the idea of logo detection before the room is asked
  # to find logos on documents. It is the
  # `book` slide's claim made a second time, in the one domain where a room
  # expects a concept to have a published spec sheet: eight results for one
  # query, arriving one at a time, and the presenter asks about each without
  # answering.
  #
  # Then how we find one, before the data built to test it (#4517): the
  # shipped matcher on real Tobacco800 pages (`make-sift-figs.py`), with a
  # FullMarks crest as the running example. The background comes first, in
  # the order a page is checked: keypoints, then matching and RANSAC. Then the
  # three things the documents changed: the keypoint budget, the shortlist and
  # why a page vector cannot build one, and the votes. Tiles shipped after
  # these slides were drawn (#4378, #4415), and the notes say so.
  outline-hold-the-line +at9
  logo-grid
  logo-keypoints
  logo-match
  logo-budget
  logo-stages
  logo-tiles
  logo-votes
  # Then the same "Data, Set" card as section VI, for the document sources and
  # FullMarks, built from the FullMarks datasheet.
  data-set-fullmarks-sources
  data-fullmarks-problems
  data-set-fullmarks
  data-fullmarks-shape
  # Last, where the line goes on documents: Goods rank, Bads set the line
  # (#4367), and tight fits hold it before the first Bad (#4440), charted from
  # the three runs' committed CSVs (`make-logo-line-fig.py`). Then the same
  # line at the other two radios: the recall end adds pages above a floor
  # under the Goods' own fits (#4458), the precision end trims to pages that
  # beat every Bad by a margin (#4479) — a schematic on the shipped constants.
  logo-line
  logo-balance
  # And how it does: the document review's two panels, one run of the app per
  # radio (`make-sota-figs.py`, from the committed per-step CSVs).
  sota-documents

  # X — Person(ification): faces, the third domain, and for now three slides: the
  # same "Data, Set" card for FHIBE (#4713), Sony's consented face benchmark,
  # which the pile's identity cells are built from (#4699). The card is drawn
  # from the release itself, which is owner-only on the GRID, so
  # `make-data-cards.py --only fhibe` runs there (see `fhibe_media.py`). How a
  # face is found and matched, where the line goes on faces and how it does
  # arrive with #4699's first study. FHIBE's terms allow a publication twenty
  # of its photos; the card's two pages show ten, so count before adding any.
  outline-hold-the-line +at10
  data-set-fhibe
  # Then Head Start: how the trained head's line is placed, the linear SVM before and after it
  # started from the Goods' average (#4776, #4785). A schematic drawn in faces, so it waits for the
  # room to meet them here; it sat after Pics on a Plane until the owner moved it (2026-10-13). What
  # it bought follows, on these faces and on COCO Better's Binary opening: Find at every vote, the
  # plain SVM against the head started from the Goods' average (#4776), drawn from the report's
  # committed curves (`make-head-start-figs.py`).
  head-start
  running-start

  # XI — Instruction: the tool as a how-to (#4202). Section I shows the same
  # session as an argument; this shows it as instructions, every click marked
  # with a numbered red disc and the numbers keyed down the side, so the pages
  # still work as a handout with nobody talking over them. One session, shot
  # once (`shoot-ui-figs.mjs`), from an empty app: load a pile of photos, make a
  # detector, train it, load a pile it never saw, and Find.
  outline-hold-the-line +at11
  steps-load-train
  steps-make-detector
  steps-train
  steps-load-test
  steps-find

  # Conclusion
  outline-hold-the-line +end

  # Appendix, for questions. First, what the work since the first rule bought
  # (#4548): each preset off its own sessions against cross-calibration, scored
  # at its own beta, on the pool thinned to 1% positive. Moved here from
  # Experimentation (#4563): only β ¼ separates, which is an answer to a question
  # rather than a slide to walk a room through.
  follow-suit
  # Then the line rules that stacked on section II's ideas and did not last,
  # moved out of Calibration (#4670); the blend went back there as the naive
  # combining (owner, 2026-10-13), and Above Average after it, as the step to
  # Curvey and InDiscreet (owner, #4833), leaving its anchored EM here. Measured in
  # F-beta at the three presets, none beat cross-calibration (#4582, at the
  # 0.44% pool). None draws today's line; the fused cut and rank transfer
  # survive only as the fallback for a session whose votes cannot support the
  # labels' model, and the 70/30 split ships as a null (#4583).
  anchored-em
  quantile-transfer
  split-fraction
  # Weight and See: how the blend's weight moves with votes (#4618). What
  # remains of the schedule is the fallback for sessions too young for the
  # folds; it is parked here pending deletion.
  blend-schedule
  # Last, the Inclusion slider's iterations — how the old user control was
  # made to mean something. Inclusion is no longer a control (#4269); it
  # survives only as the unit of the fallback acquisition cut, for a detector
  # with no labels line.
  inclusion-knob
  inclusion-walk
  inclusion-tilt
